<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  The hardened runtime's exceptions, and there are as few of them as the desk
  can be made to need — S63.

  Signing with a Developer ID turns the hardened runtime on, and notarisation
  requires it. Every key below is an *exception* to it, so each one is a small
  hole in the thing that is being asked for, and the list is short on purpose:
  `docs/manual/apple_developer_signing.md` §7 is where a new one has to argue
  for itself before it is added.

  What is deliberately **absent** is worth as much as what is here:

  * **No App Sandbox** (`com.apple.security.app-sandbox`). A sandboxed desk
    could not open a show file the operator points it at, could not reach an
    Art-Net node on the local network without a further entitlement, and could
    not spawn `prismd` at all. The App Sandbox is required for the Mac App
    Store and not for Developer ID distribution, which is what this ships as.
  * **No `disable-library-validation`.** Everything the bundle loads is signed
    by the same team, which is precisely the condition library validation
    checks. A desk that switched it off would be one that could be made to load
    somebody else's code.
  * **No network entitlements.** Those are App Sandbox keys. Outside the
    sandbox the daemon binds its loopback listener and reaches a lighting
    network without asking anyone.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!--
	  WebKit compiles JavaScript, and the interface is JavaScript. The web
	  content itself runs in Apple's own out-of-process `WKWebView` helper,
	  which is why this is the only memory-related exception here and why the
	  two blunter ones beside it in most guides — `allow-unsigned-executable-memory`
	  and `allow-dyld-environment-variables` — are not.
	-->
	<key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit</key>
	<true/>
</dict>
</plist>
